      "finding": "On-ball defensive scheme labels (DROP/ICE/SWITCH) could be assigned at confident rates of only 14.9% and 3.3% for DET and ORL respectively; switches_on was never populated, making scheme-based conditioning unreliable."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-067",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "Raw turnover-force channel lifts shrunk by ~50% or more when opponent re-route or follow-the-ball counter was accounted for; net lifts were model-implied, not observed directly, and surrogate counters often had n<10."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-012",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "Playoff defenses allowed much higher PULL_UP PPP (+0.341) than regular season, while PULL_UP share dropped 7.6 pp and PNR share jumped 12.1 pp, though tagging differences could explain part of the shift."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF13",
      "what_failed": "Within-game adaptation inference using early-third PPP to infer opponent scheme and later shift toward counter play types",
      "why": "Selection on early-third PPP extremes induced regression to the mean; no-signal baseline showed highest PPP, indicating the apparent lift was an artifact."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-001",
      "what_failed": "Falsification tests for adapter PPP lift (asymmetry, confidence, carryover)",
      "why": "Apparent adapter lift was an artifact of selection or labeling; adapter-minus-no-signal deltas were negative, and carryover test (Welch t = -1.003, p=0.842) failed to distinguish adapters."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-058",
      "what_failed": "Labeling on-ball defensive schemes (DROP/ICE/SWITCH) from matchup/PBP fields",
      "why": "Confident label rates were far below usable thresholds (14.9% and 3.3%); switches_on never populated, making scheme-based inference impossible."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Prior attempts to measure within-game adaptation lift by selecting on early possession outcomes were artefactual due to regression to the mean.",
    "Defensive scheme labeling from standard NBA data feeds is too sparse and unreliable to condition on coverage type.",
    "Apparent lifts from turnover-forcing or counter strategies shrink substantially when opponent re-routing and counters are incorporated."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No study directly tested the effect of switching to a pull-up after a failed PNR on league-wide unit-level effectiveness.",
    "The reliability of play-type labels (PNR vs PULL_UP) in the derived possession records is not evaluated; prior failures were with defensive schemes, not offensive play types.",
    "No evidence on whether a properly constructed baseline (e.g., pull-up PPP in similar situations without a prior PNR failure) would eliminate regression artefacts in this specific counter."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The question's definition of 'PNR fail' almost certainly relies on the outcome of the PNR possession (points, turnover), creating a strong regression-toward-the-mean bias, as demonstrated by SF13 and SF-APPA-001.",
    "Even if a non-outcome definition of failure could be devised (e.g., a forced pass from a trap), the data lack reliable defensive event labels, making such a definition infeasible.",
    "The unit of analysis is underspecified; likely units are teams, giving at most 30 units, which makes the 70% threshold (>=21 teams) sensitive to small-sample noise in each team's lift estimate and reduces power to detect a true median lift of 2pp.",
    "The requirement that lift be a 'real league-wide counter' calls for a causal interpretation that cannot be supported without a design that controls for defensive adjustments and endogenous play selection—neither of which is possible with only observational possession records.",
