    "The exact accuracy of derived play-type labels (PNR, PULL_UP) in the possession graph is unknown; labeling errors could add noise or bias.",
    "The available multi-season data may provide enough sequences per team to estimate unit-level lift with reasonable precision, but this is uncertain without exploratory analysis.",
    "There may exist a subset of clear PNR failures (e.g., shot-clock violations, blocked shots) that are less affected by random outcome variance, but the resulting sample size per unit would likely be very small.",
    "The hypothesis could be reinterpreted as a purely descriptive pattern (teams that use pull-ups after failed PNRs tend to have better outcomes than teams that do not), but the phrasing 'real league-wide counter' implies a causal claim that the data cannot sustain."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "Without the historical records, I would have considered the question testable with a straightforward pre-post PPP comparison within units, albeit with power concerns. The retrieval showed that nearly identical adaptation studies (SF13, SF-APPA-001) produced artefactual results due to regression to the mean and selection bias, and that defensive scheme labels are too sparse to define failure independently. This strongly shifts the assessment toward do_not_proceed, as the same pitfalls are likely to afflict a 'PNR fail then PULL_UP' lift study unless very strong controls are applied."
